WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:09.580
We decided to approach this problem by just taking apart 

00:00:09.580 --> 00:00:13.950
the system in a test tube and see if we can put it together again, 

00:00:14.410 --> 00:00:19.250
and find out what were the critical components that were needed to get membrane 

00:00:19.250 --> 00:00:22.720
organelles to move in this test tube environment.

00:00:22.940 --> 00:00:27.920
So what we did know were that microtubules were a key roadway for 

00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:30.250
this transport of membrane organelles.

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:35.720
So we knew how to prepare them from purified tubulin and we can 

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:38.390
make very pure microtubule tracks.

00:00:39.380 --> 00:00:43.960
The next thing that we needed to get was the cargo that was moving inside the axon. 

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:49.660
These are membrane organelles; these we had to get from the squid giant 

00:00:49.660 --> 00:00:54.040
axon. A third critical component was the fuel-- 

00:00:54.290 --> 00:00:57.990
adenosine triphosphate. That we knew was essential,

00:00:57.990 --> 00:01:00.600
and that we could fortunately buy from a chemical company.

00:01:01.240 --> 00:01:02.210
Most people,

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:03.150
including us,

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.700
thought that the motors would be tightly bound to the cargo,

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:11.540
so the experiment was to make all these components, put them back together 

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:13.230
in this test tube environment,

00:01:13.230 --> 00:01:17.660
put it under a microscope and see if we could see the movement of these membrane 

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:22.570
organelles occurring in this complete test tube in vitro 

00:01:22.570 --> 00:01:23.290
condition,

00:01:23.680 --> 00:01:28.140
just as if it was occurring inside of the squid giant axon itself.

00:01:28.380 --> 00:01:33.320
This experiment was originally done in 1984 at an institute called the 

00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:36.080
Marine Biological Laboratory at Woods Hole,

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:37.120
Massachusetts.

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:40.110
The day starts, actually,

00:01:40.110 --> 00:01:44.900
with fishermen going to sea and collecting squid and bring them back to us.

00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:47.450
At that point,

00:01:47.560 --> 00:01:51.950
we have the animals in hand and we can begin our preparation.

00:01:53.310 --> 00:01:54.700
So for making the tubulin,

00:01:55.180 --> 00:02:00.030
we had already protocols that were developed for making tubulin 

00:02:00.030 --> 00:02:01.370
from bovine brain,

00:02:01.920 --> 00:02:06.170
and we wanted to adapt it to the closest thing that a squid had to a brain,

00:02:06.670 --> 00:02:08.530
which is something called the optic lobe.

00:02:08.920 --> 00:02:13.580
So our strategy was to use the squid optic lobe as the 

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:18.730
biochemical source for squid tubulin, which we could then use to make 

00:02:19.010 --> 00:02:19.730
microtubules.

00:02:20.660 --> 00:02:24.530
This part here is called the squid optic lobe,

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.330
and I'm going to start by removing the squid optic lobe right 

00:02:30.350 --> 00:02:30.830
there,

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:34.980
which we'll use for our biochemical preparation of tubulin. 

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:37.630
From the squid,

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.340
we wanted to then collect the giant axon.

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:50.610
So this is the inside of the squid and now we're going to do the dissection of a 

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:51.730
squid giant axon.

00:02:53.320 --> 00:02:56.250
And this is the cartilaginous backbone of the 

00:02:58.220 --> 00:02:59.130
squid,

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:01.530
which is called the pen.

00:03:03.380 --> 00:03:07.430
And you can see it looks like an old fashioned writing pen. 

00:03:09.020 --> 00:03:12.260
I can also use this to point to different parts.

00:03:12.260 --> 00:03:17.020
So all of these lines that you see here are 

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:17.950
axons.

00:03:18.420 --> 00:03:22.330
They're all coming from this body of neurons right here.

00:03:23.260 --> 00:03:26.130
So all of these are innervating the muscle.

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:32.360
And the biggest of these axons is the squid giant axon and it lies right 

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:34.740
along the pen, right in the middle here.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:39.530
So this is the axon that we're going to have to dissect out.

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.830
There's one on either side here.

00:03:42.990 --> 00:03:45.930
So the next step is to dissect out that axon.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:50.140
We had to very carefully get the giant axon,

00:03:50.350 --> 00:03:51.340
tie it off,

00:03:51.460 --> 00:03:52.930
remove it from the squid.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:57.590
Here we are removing the giant axon and 

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:04.786
also a protective sheath that is 

00:04:04.796 --> 00:04:08.096
covering it. There,

00:04:08.976 --> 00:04:13.926
now we've got, inside of here, our giant axon, and 

00:04:13.926 --> 00:04:15.496
we'll have to clean it up later,

00:04:15.506 --> 00:04:19.586
but that is starting prep for removing it from the squid.

00:04:24.616 --> 00:04:27.986
So this is the squid giant axon right here.

00:04:29.276 --> 00:04:34.266
What I am doing is cleaning all of that sheath and kind 

00:04:34.266 --> 00:04:37.346
of material that is covering the squid giant axon.

00:04:37.806 --> 00:04:42.226
Then we needed to get the inside of the squid axon out.

00:04:42.456 --> 00:04:45.436
That can be done fairly easily just by cutting it.

00:04:46.576 --> 00:04:51.506
And then you can squeeze out the inside of the giant axon, more or less like 

00:04:51.516 --> 00:04:53.636
rolling out toothpaste from a tube.

00:04:53.736 --> 00:04:56.666
And that rolled out axoplasm,

00:04:56.676 --> 00:04:58.466
which is the inside of the squid axon,

00:04:59.006 --> 00:05:01.986
was the starting point for our preparation.

00:05:03.246 --> 00:05:04.136
From there,

00:05:04.576 --> 00:05:09.186
we had to then prepare the cargos, the membrane organelles, and separate them 

00:05:09.656 --> 00:05:11.906
from the soluble protein fraction.

00:05:12.056 --> 00:05:13.486
So inside a cell,

00:05:13.876 --> 00:05:17.226
many proteins are bound to particular compartments--

00:05:17.436 --> 00:05:19.036
a mitochondria, a nucleus,

00:05:19.566 --> 00:05:20.416
a membrane organelle--

00:05:21.946 --> 00:05:26.446
but there are also many proteins that are freely moving 

00:05:26.446 --> 00:05:30.486
around in the cytoplasm, they're unattached to anything,

00:05:30.876 --> 00:05:33.666
and these are often called cytoplasmic proteins.

00:05:33.896 --> 00:05:36.686
And when you break open the cell,

00:05:37.276 --> 00:05:41.986
they end up being in what's called a soluble protein fraction.

00:05:45.926 --> 00:05:46.936
So to do this,

00:05:46.936 --> 00:05:48.386
we took the axoplasm,

00:05:49.296 --> 00:05:50.716
put it in a buffer,

00:05:50.716 --> 00:05:52.586
which is a biochemical solution,

00:05:53.066 --> 00:05:55.986
disrupt the axon with a Pipetman,

00:05:56.936 --> 00:06:01.086
and then we place that disrupted axoplasm 

00:06:02.096 --> 00:06:06.646
on something called a sucrose gradient. A sucrose gradient is a biochemical 

00:06:06.646 --> 00:06:11.346
technique that can be used to separate, or another word is 

00:06:11.346 --> 00:06:15.166
fractionate, different components inside of a cell.

00:06:15.176 --> 00:06:17.966
Imagine taking sucrose, sugar,

00:06:18.056 --> 00:06:22.206
which may be in your kitchen and dissolving it in water,

00:06:22.976 --> 00:06:25.156
but now keep adding more and more sucrose.

00:06:25.166 --> 00:06:25.916
Eventually,

00:06:25.916 --> 00:06:28.306
it gets very viscous and thick.

00:06:29.756 --> 00:06:34.576
That was the general solution that we used in this 

00:06:34.576 --> 00:06:37.126
sucrose gradient, a very thick,

00:06:37.286 --> 00:06:41.236
viscous solution of sucrose.

00:06:42.366 --> 00:06:42.786
In fact,

00:06:42.786 --> 00:06:47.476
we made different percentages of sucrose:

00:06:48.296 --> 00:06:53.186
a very dense layer at the bottom and progressively lower density 

00:06:53.776 --> 00:06:56.206
layers higher up in this tube.

00:06:57.596 --> 00:07:02.306
We then put the axoplasmamic fraction on top of the sucrose gradient 

00:07:03.296 --> 00:07:08.066
and then put it in a centrifuge which, would spin that tube around 

00:07:08.406 --> 00:07:09.806
amazingly fast.

00:07:10.396 --> 00:07:12.006
So this is the centrifuge,

00:07:12.586 --> 00:07:17.476
and this is the rotor that 

00:07:17.486 --> 00:07:20.426
is used for spinning the sample.

00:07:21.156 --> 00:07:25.906
So here I'm going to put our sample in the bucket here,

00:07:26.596 --> 00:07:29.936
and it's very important that you have a counterweight.

00:07:30.136 --> 00:07:30.666
In fact,

00:07:30.676 --> 00:07:35.466
these two buckets have to be very carefully weighed so the rotor doesn't 

00:07:35.466 --> 00:07:36.646
become imbalanced.

00:07:37.286 --> 00:07:39.666
I'm going to put it now inside the centrifuge.

00:07:48.396 --> 00:07:51.626
And now we're ready to start centrifuging the sample.

00:07:51.626 --> 00:07:53.206
So I'll hit the start button here.

00:07:56.586 --> 00:08:01.006
And you can see the rotor now starting to spin.

00:08:01.676 --> 00:08:02.746
What happens then,

00:08:02.756 --> 00:08:07.026
is all the material in that sample start 

00:08:07.086 --> 00:08:10.826
moving down the tube through the sucrose gradient.

00:08:11.716 --> 00:08:13.666
If you're a very large object,

00:08:13.836 --> 00:08:18.756
you travel down that tube faster and that causes the membrane 

00:08:18.756 --> 00:08:20.796
organelles to form,

00:08:21.316 --> 00:08:25.826
collect there, and form a little band between that interface 

00:08:25.826 --> 00:08:29.486
between the low and the high density sucrose.

00:08:29.496 --> 00:08:34.176
And this allows us to get a very crisp band of those 

00:08:34.176 --> 00:08:38.526
membrane organelles that we could remove with a pipette 

00:08:39.216 --> 00:08:41.146
and then use in our experiments.

00:08:41.326 --> 00:08:45.896
But one thing you learn in biochemistry is to keep all of your fractions when you do an 

00:08:45.896 --> 00:08:46.546
experiment,

00:08:46.546 --> 00:08:48.426
whether you think you may need them or not.

00:08:48.916 --> 00:08:49.586
In fact,

00:08:49.586 --> 00:08:54.196
we kept other parts of that sucrose gradient, including one 

00:08:54.196 --> 00:08:58.666
fraction which was at the very top of the sucrose gradient which was the 

00:08:58.666 --> 00:09:00.586
soluble protein fractions.

00:09:01.216 --> 00:09:01.826
And again,

00:09:01.836 --> 00:09:06.726
we just put that aside and also placed it in the ice bucket, just in case we 

00:09:06.726 --> 00:09:07.586
needed it later. 

